# Editorial plan — example.com

Copy this file to `editorial-plan.md` and replace the entries with your own. It stays out of
git (it's already in `.gitignore`): it's about your site, not about this tool.

This file is the memory of the blog: what's coming, what has gone out, what was dropped and
why. It's read at the start of an interactive session to decide what to write today. The
automation uses a different, shorter file — see "The queue" at the bottom.

Convention: `[T]` technical post · `[C]` post for clients · `[P]` page. Language in brackets.
Entry titles stay in the language the piece is written in: they are the real titles.

## To write

One line per idea: the title, the angle, and — this is the part that matters — what is still
missing before it can be written. An idea that needs a number, a client name or a decision
only you can make is not ready: say so in the entry, so nobody writes it half-invented.

- [C] Why your site is slow, and what can be fixed in a day (EN).
- [T] Running containers as a non-root user: what it changes in practice (EN).
- [T] CI/CD for Laravel with Jenkins and Docker: the flow I actually use (EN).
  Needs the real pipeline steps — don't write it from memory.
- [C] ¿Cuánto cuesta una web? Lo que cambia el precio (ES). First piece for the local market.

## Interactive session only

Things that must not be published unattended: rewrites of pages that already exist, anything
touching client work, anything where a wrong call is expensive.

- [P] Services page — full rewrite (EN). It already exists online (ID 000): upload it as a
  draft and go through it together before publishing.

## Written

The record of what went out. Keep the date, the WordPress ID and the category: it's what stops
the same topic from being written twice, and it's how you find the post later.
Also note where the facts came from — a repo, a changelog, a real project — so that a year from
now it's clear the post wasn't invented.

- [T] Publishing WordPress from Markdown, over SSH (EN).
  Written and published on 2026-01-15 — ID 000, category Programming (5).
  `content/publishing-wordpress-from-markdown.md`. Facts taken from the repo itself.
  Cover generated (Cloudflare) and set on 2026-01-15, attachment id=000.

- [C] Brochure site or e-commerce: how to tell what your business needs (EN).
  Written on 2026-01-10, **published on 2026-01-11** — ID 000, category Websites (00).
  `content/brochure-site-or-ecommerce.md`.

## Dropped

Ideas that didn't hold up, with the reason. They live here so they don't get proposed again in
six months, and so that it's clear what would have to change for them to come back.

- [C] How much does a website cost in 2026 (EN). Needs real price ranges: without them the post
  betrays its own title, and making them up would mean publishing commercial terms that don't
  exist. Move it back to "To write" when the numbers are there.

## Open technical notes

Optional, but useful: problems found while publishing and how they were solved. Strike through
the ones that are fixed instead of deleting them — it's the fastest way to avoid debugging the
same thing twice.

- ~~Uploads failed with a database error on non-ASCII characters~~ **Fixed on 2026-01-12.**
  It was the stdin encoding on Windows; `publish.py:wp()` now forces UTF-8.

## The queue

The daily automation (`daily.cmd`) does **not** read this file: it reads a shorter queue file
with three sections — Queue, Published, Skipped — takes the first entry and moves it to
Published once the post is online. Keep the two separate: this plan is where *you* think,
the queue is what the automation consumes. When an idea from here is ready to be written
unattended, copy it into the queue.
